add("can you explain the oauth 2.0 authorization flows and when to use each one?",
    "OAuth 2.0 defines several authorization flows (grant types), each designed for different scenarios. Let me walk through the main ones.\n\nAuthorization Code Flow: The most secure and common flow for server-side web apps. The client redirects the user to the authorization server, the user logs in and consents, the auth server redirects back with an authorization code, and the client exchanges that code for tokens server-side. The tokens never touch the browser. Use this for traditional web applications with a backend.\n\nAuthorization Code Flow with PKCE (Proof Key for Code Exchange): An extension of the above for public clients (SPAs, mobile apps) that can't securely store a client secret. The client generates a random code_verifier and sends its SHA-256 hash (code_challenge) with the auth request. When exchanging the code for tokens, it sends the original code_verifier. The auth server verifies the hash matches. This prevents authorization code interception attacks. Use this for SPAs and mobile apps. It's now recommended for ALL clients, even confidential ones.\n\nClient Credentials Flow: For machine-to-machine communication where no user is involved. The client authenticates with its client_id and client_secret and gets an access token directly. Use this for microservice-to-microservice calls, background jobs, and CLIs.\n\nDevice Authorization Flow (Device Code): For devices with limited input capabilities (smart TVs, IoT devices). The device shows a URL and code, the user visits the URL on another device and enters the code, and the device polls until authorization completes. Use this for IoT, smart TVs, and CLI tools.\n\nThe Implicit Flow (returning tokens directly in the redirect URL) is deprecated. It was designed for SPAs before PKCE existed but is insecure because tokens are exposed in browser history and URLs. Resource Owner Password Credentials (ROPC) flow is also deprecated as it requires the client to handle user passwords directly, defeating the purpose of OAuth.\n\nKey tokens: Access tokens (short-lived, used to access APIs), Refresh tokens (long-lived, used to get new access tokens), and ID tokens (from OpenID Connect, contain user identity claims).",
    "oauth 2.0, authorization code, pkce, client credentials, device flow, implicit deprecated, access refresh tokens, openid connect")

add("how do jwts work internally and what are common security pitfalls?",
    "JSON Web Tokens (JWTs) are a compact, self-contained way to represent claims between parties. Understanding their internals helps you avoid the many security pitfalls.\n\nA JWT has three parts separated by dots: header.payload.signature. The header specifies the algorithm (e.g., RS256, HS256) and token type. The payload contains claims: registered claims (iss, sub, exp, iat, aud, nbf, jti), public claims, and private claims. The signature is created by signing the encoded header and payload with a secret (HMAC) or private key (RSA/ECDSA).\n\nImportant: JWTs are encoded (Base64URL), NOT encrypted. Anyone can decode and read the payload. The signature only ensures integrity (it hasn't been tampered with) and authenticity (it was issued by someone with the secret/private key).\n\nCommon security pitfalls:\n\n1. Algorithm confusion (alg:none): Some libraries accept tokens with 'alg':'none', meaning no signature verification. Always validate the algorithm and reject 'none'.\n\n2. Algorithm switching (RS256 to HS256): If a server uses RS256 (asymmetric), an attacker might forge a token using HS256 (symmetric) with the public key as the HMAC secret. Libraries should be configured to accept only specific algorithms.\n\n3. No expiration: Always set 'exp' and validate it. JWTs without expiration are valid forever if the signing key isn't rotated.\n\n4. Storing sensitive data: Since JWTs aren't encrypted, never put passwords, SSNs, or secrets in the payload. Use JWE (JSON Web Encryption) if you need encrypted tokens.\n\n5. Token storage in the browser: localStorage is vulnerable to XSS. httpOnly cookies are better but require CSRF protection. There's no perfect solution; each has tradeoffs.\n\n6. No revocation mechanism: JWTs are stateless, so you can't invalidate them before expiration. Mitigations include short expiration times (15 minutes), token blacklists (defeats the stateless benefit), or refresh token rotation.\n\n7. Large tokens: JWTs in cookies have a 4KB limit. In headers, large tokens increase bandwidth for every request. Keep claims minimal.\n\nBest practices: use RS256 or ES256 over HS256, keep tokens short-lived, validate all claims (exp, iss, aud), use a well-maintained library, and rotate signing keys periodically.",
    "jwt internals, header payload signature, algorithm confusion, rs256 hs256, token security, claims, xss csrf, stateless revocation")

add("what is cors and how does it actually work under the hood?",
    "CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) is a browser security mechanism that controls how web pages can make requests to different origins. It's one of the most misunderstood web concepts, so let me break it down.\n\nFirst, the Same-Origin Policy: browsers restrict web pages from making requests to a different origin (protocol + domain + port). https://example.com:443 and https://api.example.com:443 are different origins. Without SOP, any website could read your Gmail by making fetch requests with your cookies.\n\nCORS is a controlled relaxation of SOP. The server tells the browser which cross-origin requests are allowed via HTTP headers.\n\nSimple requests (GET, HEAD, POST with simple content types) go directly to the server. The browser adds an 'Origin' header. If the server responds with 'Access-Control-Allow-Origin' matching that origin (or '*'), the browser allows the response. Otherwise, the browser blocks it. Note: the request was still made and processed; the browser just hides the response from JavaScript.\n\nPreflight requests happen for 'non-simple' requests (PUT, DELETE, custom headers, JSON content-type). Before the actual request, the browser sends an OPTIONS request with 'Access-Control-Request-Method' and 'Access-Control-Request-Headers'. The server responds with what's allowed: 'Access-Control-Allow-Methods', 'Access-Control-Allow-Headers', 'Access-Control-Max-Age' (how long to cache the preflight). Only if the preflight succeeds does the browser send the actual request.\n\nCredentialed requests (with cookies or auth headers) require 'Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true' AND a specific origin (not '*'). This is a common gotcha.\n\nCommon mistakes: Setting 'Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *' with credentials (doesn't work). Reflecting the Origin header without validation (security risk: any site can access your API). Not handling OPTIONS method in your server. Forgetting to expose custom headers with 'Access-Control-Expose-Headers'. Debugging CORS by disabling it in the browser (hides real issues).\n\nImportant: CORS is enforced by browsers only. Server-to-server requests, Postman, and curl don't care about CORS. It protects users, not APIs. For API security, use authentication and authorization independently of CORS.",
    "cors, cross origin resource sharing, same origin policy, preflight options, simple requests, credentials, browser security, access control headers")

add("can you walk through what happens during a tls handshake?",
    "The TLS handshake is what establishes a secure, encrypted connection between a client and server. Here's what happens step by step, using TLS 1.3 (the current version) as the primary example.\n\nTLS 1.3 Handshake (1-RTT):\n\n1. Client Hello: The client sends supported cipher suites, a random number, supported TLS versions, and key shares for supported key exchange groups (like X25519 or P-256). In TLS 1.3, the client guesses which key exchange the server will prefer and sends key shares upfront. This is what makes 1-RTT possible.\n\n2. Server Hello: The server selects the cipher suite, sends its random number, and its key share. At this point, both sides can compute the shared secret using ECDHE (Elliptic Curve Diffie-Hellman Ephemeral). The server also sends its certificate, a Certificate Verify message (proving it has the private key for the certificate), and a Finished message, all encrypted with the handshake keys.\n\n3. Client Finished: The client verifies the server's certificate against its trust store (checking the certificate chain up to a trusted root CA, expiration, revocation via OCSP/CRL, and that the domain matches). If valid, it sends its Finished message. Application data can now flow.\n\nTLS 1.3 improvements over 1.2: Only 1 round trip (1.2 required 2). Removed insecure features: no RSA key exchange (only ECDHE for forward secrecy), no CBC mode ciphers, no SHA-1, no compression. 0-RTT resumption: if the client has connected before, it can send application data in the first message using a pre-shared key. However, 0-RTT data is vulnerable to replay attacks, so it should only be used for idempotent requests.\n\nCertificate pinning: Instead of trusting any certificate signed by any CA, the client 'pins' to specific certificates or public keys. This prevents attacks where a compromised CA issues fraudulent certificates. Used in mobile apps and critical infrastructure. However, it's risky: if you lose your pinned key and don't have backup pins, your app is bricked. HPKP (HTTP Public Key Pinning) was deprecated because of this. Certificate Transparency logs are now the preferred approach.\n\nThe result of the handshake: both sides agree on a cipher suite (e.g., TLS_AES_256_GCM_SHA384), have derived symmetric session keys (one for each direction), and the client has verified the server's identity. All subsequent data is encrypted with the session keys using symmetric encryption (AES-GCM or ChaCha20-Poly1305).",
    "tls handshake, tls 1.3, ecdhe, forward secrecy, certificate verification, 0-rtt, certificate pinning, cipher suites, encryption")

add("what is content security policy and how does it prevent xss attacks?",
    "Content Security Policy (CSP) is an HTTP security header that helps prevent Cross-Site Scripting (XSS), clickjacking, and other code injection attacks by specifying which sources of content the browser should trust.\n\nHow it works: The server sends a Content-Security-Policy header with directives that define allowed sources for different content types. The browser enforces these rules and blocks anything that violates them. If an attacker injects a malicious script, the browser refuses to execute it because it doesn't match the policy.\n\nKey directives: default-src sets the fallback for all resource types. script-src controls JavaScript sources. style-src controls CSS sources. img-src controls image sources. connect-src controls fetch, XHR, and WebSocket destinations. font-src, media-src, frame-src control their respective resource types. form-action restricts where forms can submit. frame-ancestors replaces X-Frame-Options for clickjacking prevention.\n\nSource values: 'self' allows same-origin resources. 'none' blocks everything. Specific domains like 'https://cdn.example.com'. 'unsafe-inline' allows inline scripts/styles (try to avoid). 'unsafe-eval' allows eval() and similar (definitely avoid). 'nonce-{random}' allows specific inline scripts with a matching nonce attribute. 'strict-dynamic' trusts scripts loaded by already-trusted scripts. Hash values allow specific inline scripts by their hash.\n\nModern best practice is a strict CSP using nonces: Content-Security-Policy: script-src 'nonce-abc123' 'strict-dynamic'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'none'. Each page load generates a random nonce. Only script tags with that nonce execute. strict-dynamic allows those trusted scripts to load additional scripts. This is Google's recommended approach.\n\nDeployment strategy: Start with Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only to monitor violations without blocking. Analyze reports (configure report-uri or report-to directive). Fix violations by moving inline scripts to files or adding nonces. Switch to enforcing mode. Gradually tighten the policy.\n\nCommon pitfalls: Using 'unsafe-inline' defeats most of CSP's XSS protection. Overly permissive policies (default-src *) provide no real security. Forgetting about inline event handlers (onclick, onerror) which CSP blocks by default. Not setting object-src 'none' (Flash/Java plugins can bypass other protections).",
    "content security policy, csp, xss prevention, script-src, nonce, strict dynamic, report only, browser security, directives, inline scripts")

