add("what are lazy loading and code splitting and how do they improve web performance?",
    "Lazy loading and code splitting are two critical performance optimization techniques that reduce the initial load time of web applications.\n\nLazy loading defers the loading of resources until they're actually needed. Instead of loading everything upfront, you load things on demand. For images: use the native loading='lazy' attribute or Intersection Observer API to load images only when they scroll into view. This can save megabytes of bandwidth on image-heavy pages. For components: React.lazy() and Suspense let you render a component lazily. The component's code is only fetched when it's first rendered. For routes: load page components only when the user navigates to them. This is the most impactful form of lazy loading in SPAs.\n\nCode splitting breaks your JavaScript bundle into smaller chunks that are loaded on demand. Without code splitting, your entire app is one massive bundle. Users download megabytes of JavaScript they might never use. With code splitting, you create separate chunks for different routes, features, or libraries.\n\nWebpack implements code splitting through: dynamic imports (import('./module').then(m => ...)), which create new chunks automatically. Entry points: multiple entry configurations. SplitChunksPlugin: automatically splits vendor code (node_modules) into separate chunks for better caching.\n\nReact-specific patterns: Route-based splitting is the easiest win. Wrap each route component with React.lazy(). Component-based splitting for heavy components like charts, editors, or maps. Library-based splitting: keep large libraries like moment.js or lodash in separate chunks.\n\nCommon strategy: Load the critical path (initial route) immediately. Prefetch likely next routes during idle time (rel='prefetch'). Lazy load everything below the fold. Split vendor chunks for long-term caching.\n\nMeasuring impact: Use Lighthouse, WebPageTest, or Chrome DevTools Coverage tab to see how much JavaScript is unused. A good target is under 200KB of JavaScript for the initial load (compressed). Use bundle analyzers (webpack-bundle-analyzer, source-map-explorer) to visualize what's in your bundles.\n\nThe combination of lazy loading and code splitting can reduce initial page load by 50-80% in large applications. It's one of the highest-impact optimizations you can make.",
    "lazy loading, code splitting, react lazy, dynamic imports, webpack, bundle optimization, prefetch, intersection observer, initial load")

add("what are core web vitals and how do you optimize them?",
    "Core Web Vitals are Google's metrics for measuring real-world user experience on the web. They directly impact SEO rankings and, more importantly, user satisfaction.\n\nThe three Core Web Vitals:\n\nLargest Contentful Paint (LCP): measures loading performance. It's the time until the largest visible content element (image, video, text block) is rendered. Target: under 2.5 seconds. Optimization: optimize the critical rendering path, preload the LCP element, use CDN for assets, optimize images (proper sizing, modern formats like WebP/AVIF, responsive images), reduce server response time (TTFB), avoid render-blocking resources, and use server-side rendering or static generation.\n\nInteraction to Next Paint (INP): replaced First Input Delay in March 2024. Measures responsiveness throughout the page lifecycle, not just the first interaction. It's the worst-case input latency (excluding outliers). Target: under 200ms. Optimization: break long tasks into smaller ones using requestIdleCallback or scheduler.yield(), minimize main thread work, defer non-critical JavaScript, use web workers for heavy computation, optimize event handlers (debounce, throttle), reduce JavaScript execution time, and avoid layout thrashing.\n\nCumulative Layout Shift (CLS): measures visual stability. It quantifies how much the page layout shifts unexpectedly during loading. Target: under 0.1. Optimization: always set width and height on images and videos (or use aspect-ratio CSS), reserve space for ads and embeds, avoid inserting content above existing content, use transform animations instead of layout-triggering properties, preload fonts and use font-display: swap with size-adjust to minimize shift, and use contain: layout on dynamic content areas.\n\nTools for measurement: Chrome DevTools Performance panel, Lighthouse (lab data), PageSpeed Insights (combines lab and field data), Chrome UX Report (CrUX, real user data), web-vitals JavaScript library for RUM (Real User Monitoring).\n\nPro tips: Field data (from real users) matters more than lab data for rankings. Focus on the 75th percentile, not the median. Mobile performance is usually the bottleneck. Test on real devices, not just DevTools throttling. Performance budgets help prevent regressions: fail CI if LCP exceeds your target.",
    "core web vitals, lcp, inp, cls, web performance, lighthouse, rum, loading responsiveness stability, seo ranking, optimization")

add("how does tree shaking work and why is it important for bundle size?",
    "Tree shaking is a dead code elimination technique used by modern JavaScript bundlers to remove unused exports from your final bundle. The name comes from the metaphor of shaking a tree and letting the dead leaves fall off.\n\nHow it works: Tree shaking relies on the static structure of ES modules (import/export). Unlike CommonJS require() which is dynamic and can be conditional, ES module imports are declarations that can be analyzed at build time. The bundler builds a dependency graph starting from your entry point, traces which exports are actually imported and used, and marks unused exports as 'dead code' that can be safely removed.\n\nFor example, if lodash-es exports 300 functions and you only import 'debounce', tree shaking removes the other 299 functions from your bundle. This can save hundreds of kilobytes.\n\nWhy ES modules matter: CommonJS (require) is dynamic and can't be statically analyzed. require('./utils')[someVariable] could import anything. That's why tree shaking only works with ES module syntax. This is why many libraries now publish both CJS and ESM versions, and why you should prefer the ESM version.\n\nWebpack and Rollup implement tree shaking differently. Webpack marks unused exports and relies on Terser (minifier) to actually remove the dead code. Rollup includes only what's needed from the start, making it naturally more aggressive. Vite uses Rollup for production builds.\n\nCommon gotchas that break tree shaking: Side effects: if a module has top-level code that runs on import (logging, polyfills, CSS imports), the bundler can't remove it even if no exports are used. The 'sideEffects' field in package.json tells the bundler which files are safe to tree-shake. Re-exports: barrel files (index.js that re-exports everything) can prevent tree shaking if the bundler can't trace through them efficiently. Class methods can't be tree-shaken because the bundler can't know if they're called dynamically. Dynamic access patterns like obj[key] prevent analysis.\n\nBest practices: use ES module imports exclusively, prefer named imports over namespace imports (import { x } from 'y' over import * as y), set 'sideEffects: false' in your package.json if your library is pure, avoid barrel files for large libraries, use a bundle analyzer to verify tree shaking is working, and consider replacing heavy libraries with lighter alternatives (date-fns instead of moment.js).",
    "tree shaking, dead code elimination, es modules, static analysis, webpack rollup, side effects, bundle size, named imports, barrel files")

