add("how do b-trees work and why are they used in databases?",
    "B-trees are self-balancing tree data structures that maintain sorted data and allow searches, insertions, and deletions in O(log n) time. They're the backbone of almost every database index and filesystem.\n\nWhy not binary search trees? BSTs have O(log n) height, but each node access is a disk I/O operation. A BST with 1 million nodes has ~20 levels = 20 disk reads per search. Disk reads are incredibly slow (milliseconds vs nanoseconds for memory). B-trees solve this by being wide and short: each node can have hundreds or thousands of children, so the tree is only 3-4 levels deep even for billions of records.\n\nB-tree properties (order m): Each node has at most m children and m-1 keys. Each internal node (except root) has at least ceil(m/2) children. All leaves are at the same level. Keys within a node are sorted.\n\nA B-tree of order 1000 with 1 billion records has only 3-4 levels. That's 3-4 disk reads to find any record. Each node is sized to match a disk page (typically 4KB-16KB), maximizing the data read per I/O.\n\nB+ trees (used by most databases, including PostgreSQL, MySQL InnoDB, SQLite): All data is stored in leaf nodes only. Internal nodes only store keys for routing. Leaf nodes are linked in a linked list for efficient range queries. This means scanning all records between 'A' and 'M' only requires finding 'A' in the tree (3-4 reads) and then following leaf pointers sequentially, which is very cache-friendly.\n\nOperations: Search traverses from root, picking the right child at each level. Insertion finds the correct leaf and adds the key. If the leaf is full, it splits into two and pushes the middle key up. This might cascade up to the root. Deletion removes the key and may cause underflow, triggering merges or redistributions.\n\nWhy databases love B+ trees: predictable performance (always O(log n)), optimized for disk I/O patterns, excellent for range queries, good cache utilization (sequential leaf scanning), support concurrent access with page-level locking, and remain balanced automatically.\n\nAlternatives: LSM trees (used by RocksDB, LevelDB, Cassandra) are better for write-heavy workloads. They buffer writes in memory and periodically flush sorted runs to disk, achieving better write throughput at the cost of more complex reads.",
    "b-tree, b+ tree, database index, disk io, balanced tree, range queries, leaf nodes, order, splitting, lsm tree comparison")

add("what are bloom filters and how are they useful in practice?",
    "A Bloom filter is a space-efficient probabilistic data structure that tells you whether an element is 'possibly in the set' or 'definitely not in the set.' It can have false positives but never false negatives.\n\nHow it works: You have a bit array of m bits, all initially set to 0, and k independent hash functions. To add an element, hash it with all k functions and set those k bit positions to 1. To query an element, hash it with all k functions and check if all k positions are 1. If any bit is 0, the element is definitely not in the set. If all bits are 1, the element is probably in the set (but might be a false positive due to collisions).\n\nThe math: For a Bloom filter with m bits, k hash functions, and n elements, the false positive rate is approximately (1 - e^(-kn/m))^k. The optimal number of hash functions is k = (m/n) * ln(2). For a 1% false positive rate, you need about 9.6 bits per element. For 0.1%, about 14.4 bits per element. This is incredibly space-efficient compared to storing the actual elements.\n\nReal-world applications: Databases: Cassandra and HBase use Bloom filters to avoid disk reads. Before reading an SSTable file, check the Bloom filter. If it says the key isn't there, skip the file entirely. This massively reduces I/O. Web browsers: Chrome uses Bloom filters for safe browsing. It checks URLs against a local Bloom filter before sending them to Google's servers. CDN/Caching: check if content is likely cached before making an expensive lookup. One-hit-wonder filtering: before adding something to a cache, check a Bloom filter. Only cache items that appear in the filter (have been seen before). This prevents cache pollution from infrequent items. Spell checkers: quickly check if a word is in the dictionary. Network routers: check if a packet matches filter rules. Blockchain: Bitcoin SPV nodes use Bloom filters to request only relevant transactions.\n\nVariants: Counting Bloom filters use counters instead of bits, supporting deletion. Cuckoo filters offer better performance for deletion and can be more space-efficient at low false positive rates. Scalable Bloom filters dynamically grow as elements are added.\n\nLimitations: You can't enumerate the elements, can't remove elements (without counting variant), and the false positive rate increases as more elements are added.",
    "bloom filter, probabilistic, false positives, bit array, hash functions, space efficient, cassandra, caching, counting bloom filter")

add("what is consistent hashing and why is it important for distributed systems?",
    "Consistent hashing is a technique for distributing data across a cluster of nodes in a way that minimizes redistribution when nodes are added or removed. It's fundamental to distributed databases, caches, and load balancers.\n\nThe problem with simple hashing: If you use hash(key) % N to assign keys to N servers, and you add or remove a server (N changes), almost all keys get remapped. For a cache cluster, this means nearly 100% cache miss rate during scaling. Catastrophic.\n\nConsistent hashing solution: Imagine a ring (hash space from 0 to 2^32-1). Each node is hashed to a position on the ring. Each key is hashed to a position on the ring and assigned to the first node clockwise from its position. When you add a node, only the keys between the new node and its predecessor need to move (roughly 1/N of all keys). When you remove a node, only its keys move to the next node. This is dramatically better than simple hashing.\n\nThe problem with basic consistent hashing: if nodes are unevenly distributed on the ring, some nodes get more keys than others (hotspots). Solution: virtual nodes (vnodes). Each physical node gets multiple positions on the ring (e.g., 150-256 virtual nodes each). This creates a much more even distribution. When a node is added, it takes small portions of data from many nodes rather than a large chunk from one.\n\nReal-world usage: Amazon DynamoDB uses consistent hashing with virtual nodes for partitioning. Apache Cassandra uses it for distributing data across the ring. Memcached clients use it for distributing cache keys. CDNs use it for routing requests to edge servers. Discord uses it for distributing chat servers.\n\nRelated concepts: Rendezvous hashing (highest random weight) is an alternative where each key computes a weight for every node and picks the highest. It's simpler to implement and handles node weights naturally, but requires checking all nodes per key. Jump consistent hashing (from Google) provides perfectly even distribution with minimal memory usage, but only works when nodes are numbered 0 to N-1.\n\nConsistent hashing is one of those algorithms that seems simple but has profound implications. It's why distributed systems can scale horizontally by just adding nodes without massive data reorganization.",
    "consistent hashing, hash ring, virtual nodes, vnodes, data distribution, dynamodb cassandra, minimal redistribution, load balancing")

add("what are tries and when would you use them over hash maps?",
    "A trie (pronounced 'tree' or 'try', from reTRIEval) is a tree data structure where each node represents a character in a string. The path from root to a node spells out a prefix, and nodes can be marked as end-of-word.\n\nStructure: The root is empty. Each edge represents a character. Each node has up to alphabet_size children (26 for lowercase English). A boolean flag marks which nodes represent complete words. For example, storing 'cat', 'car', 'card': root -> c -> a -> t (end), and a -> r (end) -> d (end).\n\nTime complexity: Insert and search are O(L) where L is the word length, regardless of how many words are stored. This is the key advantage. A hash map also has O(L) for hashing the string, but a trie gives you much more.\n\nWhen tries beat hash maps:\n\nPrefix search: 'Find all words starting with pre-' is trivial in a trie (navigate to the prefix node, then enumerate all descendants). Hash maps can't do this without checking every key.\n\nAutocomplete: Given a partial input, you can efficiently find all completions. This is how search engines, IDEs, and phone keyboards work. You traverse to the prefix node and perform BFS/DFS to find completions, optionally ranked by frequency.\n\nSpell checking: Find words within edit distance K of a query. Tries can prune large portions of the search space during traversal.\n\nLongest prefix matching: Used in IP routing tables (longest prefix match for routing decisions) and URL routing. Tries find the longest matching prefix in O(L) time.\n\nSorted iteration: Tries naturally iterate in lexicographic order. Hash maps have no inherent ordering.\n\nWhen hash maps are better: Simple exact lookups where you don't need prefix operations. When memory is constrained and the alphabet is large (each trie node can use significant memory). When strings are long and varied (the trie becomes very deep with little sharing).\n\nOptimizations: Compressed tries (radix trees/Patricia trees) merge nodes with single children, dramatically reducing memory usage. For the string 'romane', 'romanus', 'romulus', instead of one node per character, you get 'rom' -> 'an' -> 'e'/'us' and 'ulus'. Ternary search tries use three children per node (less, equal, greater) for better memory usage with large alphabets. Array-mapped tries (used in Clojure and Scala's persistent data structures) provide efficient immutable collections.",
    "trie, prefix tree, autocomplete, prefix search, radix tree, patricia tree, ip routing, spell checking, hash map comparison")

# --- Functional Programming ---

add("what is immutability and why does it matter in programming?",
    "Immutability means that once a value is created, it cannot be changed. Instead of modifying existing data, you create new data with the desired changes. This seemingly simple concept has profound implications for software correctness and reliability.\n\nWhy immutability matters:\n\nConcurrency safety: Mutable shared state is the root cause of most concurrency bugs (race conditions, deadlocks, inconsistent reads). If data can't be changed, it's inherently thread-safe. No locks, no synchronization, no data races. This is why Erlang (which powers WhatsApp's backend serving billions of messages) uses immutable data exclusively.\n\nPredictability: When you pass an object to a function, you know it won't be modified. No defensive copying needed. No surprises. You can reason about your code locally without worrying about distant code mutating your data.\n\nTime travel debugging: If you keep previous versions of state (since you never modify, only create new), you can inspect or restore any previous state. Redux in React uses this principle for time-travel debugging. Event sourcing in backend systems works on the same idea.\n\nCaching and memoization: If inputs can't change, function results can be safely cached. React.memo, useMemo, and PureComponent all rely on reference equality checks that work because immutable updates create new references.\n\nPractical implementation: In JavaScript, use Object.freeze() (shallow), spread operators for shallow copies, or libraries like Immer (which lets you write 'mutable' code that produces immutable updates) or Immutable.js. In Python, use tuples instead of lists, frozensets instead of sets, and namedtuples. In Java, use final fields, unmodifiable collections, and records (Java 16+).\n\nPerformance concern: Creating new objects for every change seems wasteful, but persistent data structures use structural sharing. When you 'modify' a node in a tree, only the path from that node to the root is copied; everything else is shared with the old version. This makes immutable updates O(log n) instead of O(n). Clojure's persistent vectors and maps use this technique brilliantly.\n\nThe balance: Full immutability everywhere can be impractical. Use immutability for shared state, public APIs, and data flowing through your system. Use mutability for local, encapsulated state where performance matters (tight loops, algorithms).",
    "immutability, thread safety, concurrency, structural sharing, persistent data structures, redux, immer, defensive copying, referential transparency")

add("what are monads and why are they useful beyond just being a meme?",
    "Monads are one of the most feared concepts in programming, but they're actually a practical pattern you probably already use without knowing it. Let me demystify them.\n\nA monad is a design pattern for composing functions that return 'wrapped' values. Formally, it's a type M that has two operations: unit (also called return, of, or pure): takes a regular value and wraps it. a -> M(a). bind (also called flatMap, chain, or >>=): takes a wrapped value and a function that returns a wrapped value, and combines them. M(a) -> (a -> M(b)) -> M(b).\n\nThese operations must follow three laws: left identity, right identity, and associativity. But let's focus on why they're useful.\n\nMonads you already use:\n\nPromise/Future: Promise.resolve(5) is 'unit'. .then() is 'bind'. Promises let you chain asynchronous operations without nested callbacks. promise.then(x => fetchUser(x)).then(user => fetchOrders(user)). Each function returns a Promise, and .then() handles the unwrapping.\n\nOptional/Maybe: Instead of null checks everywhere, wrap values in Maybe. Maybe.of(user).flatMap(u => u.address).flatMap(a => a.zipCode). If any step is null/undefined, the chain short-circuits to Nothing. No null pointer exceptions. Java's Optional, Swift's Optional, and Rust's Option all implement this.\n\nResult/Either: Like Maybe but carries error information. Instead of throwing exceptions, return Result<Value, Error>. Chain operations with flatMap. Errors propagate automatically. Rust's Result type is the best mainstream example.\n\nList/Array: Array.of(1,2,3).flatMap(x => [x, x*2]) = [1,2,2,4,3,6]. flatMap lets you chain operations that produce multiple results.\n\nIO Monad: In Haskell, all side effects (console output, file I/O, network requests) are wrapped in the IO monad. This makes it explicit which functions have side effects and which are pure. The type system enforces this separation.\n\nWhy monads matter: They provide a unified interface for handling context (async, nullability, errors, state, logging) while keeping your business logic clean. Instead of special syntax for each concern (try/catch for errors, async/await for promises, null checks for optionals), monads give you one composable pattern: flatMap.\n\nThe key insight: monads are about sequential composition with context. Each step in the chain can use the result of the previous step, and the monad handles the plumbing (unwrapping, error propagation, async scheduling) so you focus on the logic.",
    "monads, flatmap, bind, promise, optional maybe, result either, io monad, composition, functional programming, haskell, chain")

add("how does currying work and what's the difference between currying and partial application?",
    "Currying and partial application are related but distinct functional programming techniques. Both involve breaking down multi-argument functions, but they work differently.\n\nCurrying transforms a function that takes multiple arguments into a series of functions that each take a single argument. A function f(a, b, c) becomes f(a)(b)(c). Each call returns a new function that takes the next argument.\n\nExample in JavaScript: const add = a => b => a + b. add(3)(5) returns 8. add(3) returns a function that adds 3 to whatever you give it. const addThree = add(3); addThree(5) is 8, addThree(10) is 13.\n\nPartial application fixes some arguments of a function and returns a new function with fewer arguments. Unlike currying, it doesn't require one-argument-at-a-time. You can fix any number of arguments at once.\n\nExample: const multiply = (a, b, c) => a * b * c. const double = multiply.bind(null, 2); double(3, 4) returns 24. We fixed the first argument to 2 and got a function taking 2 arguments.\n\nKey difference: Currying always produces unary (single-argument) functions in a chain. Partial application can produce functions of any arity. Currying is a specific transformation of the function's structure. Partial application is about providing some arguments early.\n\nWhy currying is useful:\n\nFunction composition: Curried functions compose naturally. const compose = (f, g) => x => f(g(x)). If map, filter, and sort are curried, you can build data pipelines: const process = compose(map(transform), filter(isValid)).\n\nPoint-free style: Instead of data => data.map(x => x * 2), curried functions let you write map(multiply(2)). The data argument is implicit, making the code more declarative.\n\nReusable specialized functions: const log = level => message => console.log(`[${level}] ${message}`). const info = log('INFO'), error = log('ERROR'). info('Server started'), error('Connection failed').\n\nConfiguration: const createFetcher = baseUrl => endpoint => params => fetch(`${baseUrl}${endpoint}?${params}`). const api = createFetcher('https://api.example.com'). const getUsers = api('/users'). Now getUsers('limit=10') makes the full request.\n\nLanguages with native currying: Haskell (all functions are curried by default), F#, OCaml, Elm. In JavaScript, libraries like Ramda and lodash/fp provide auto-currying utilities.\n\nPractical tip: Currying is most valuable when you're doing a lot of function composition and building pipelines. For everyday code, partial application with .bind() or arrow functions is usually more readable and practical.",
    "currying, partial application, unary functions, function composition, point-free style, ramda, haskell, higher order functions, specialization")

