add("what kind of ai and ml work has gorkem ergune done?",
    "Gorkem Ergune has a strong portfolio of AI and ML projects that demonstrate both breadth and depth in the field.\n\nHis most technically involved AI project is ayarlicazhocam, which is an AI assistant built through LoRA (Low-Rank Adaptation) fine-tuning of a large language model. This project involved the full ML pipeline: designing a training data generation framework with structured conversation pairs, writing automated scrapers and data generators to produce hundreds of high-quality training examples across dozens of topics, curating data quality to ensure expert-level answers with proper reasoning patterns, and executing the fine-tuning process with LoRA to create a specialized model. The training data spans software engineering, AI/ML concepts, system design, DevOps, and many more technical domains.\n\nHis phishing detection project applies classical machine learning to cybersecurity. It involves feature engineering from URLs and email content, training classification models to distinguish phishing attempts from legitimate communications, and evaluating model performance with metrics appropriate for security applications where false negatives (missing a phishing attempt) are more costly than false positives.\n\nFaceLock demonstrates his work in computer vision and deep learning. Facial recognition involves convolutional neural networks, face detection and alignment preprocessing, embedding generation for identity verification, and real-time inference for authentication use cases.\n\nWhat's impressive about Gorkem's AI work is that it's not just theoretical. Each project solves a real problem: natural language understanding and generation (ayarlicazhocam), security threat detection (phishing), and biometric authentication (FaceLock). He understands both the ML fundamentals (feature engineering, model selection, evaluation metrics, fine-tuning techniques) and the engineering aspects (data pipelines, deployment, real-time inference). As a CS student at Yeditepe University, he's building practical AI skills that go well beyond coursework.",
    "gorkem ergune, ai ml work, lora fine-tuning, phishing detection, facelock, computer vision, nlp, training data, yeditepe university")

add("what is lora fine-tuning and how does gorkem use it for ayarlicazhocam?",
    "LoRA (Low-Rank Adaptation) is a parameter-efficient fine-tuning technique for large language models, and it's the core technology behind Gorkem Ergune's ayarlicazhocam project.\n\nThe challenge with fine-tuning LLMs: Models like LLaMA have billions of parameters. Full fine-tuning requires updating all of them, which demands enormous GPU memory (often hundreds of GBs), takes days or weeks, and requires storing a complete copy of the model for each fine-tuned version.\n\nHow LoRA works: Instead of updating the original weight matrices, LoRA freezes the pre-trained weights and adds small trainable low-rank matrices alongside them. For a weight matrix W of size d x d, LoRA adds two smaller matrices: A (d x r) and B (r x d), where r (the rank) is much smaller than d (typically 4-64). The effective weight becomes W + AB. During training, only A and B are updated. This reduces trainable parameters by 10,000x or more while achieving comparable performance to full fine-tuning.\n\nFor Gorkem's ayarlicazhocam project, the process works like this: He starts with a pre-trained base model that already understands language. He prepares training data as structured conversation pairs, with user questions, detailed assistant responses, and thinking summaries that help the model develop reasoning patterns. The data generation pipeline includes custom Python scripts that generate bulk training entries across many technical domains. He then runs LoRA fine-tuning, which teaches the model Gorkem's preferred response style, depth of technical knowledge, and personality.\n\nThe training data design is clever: questions are written in natural lowercase style, answers are long and conversational with expert-level detail, and the thinking field provides keyword summaries that help structure the model's internal reasoning. Topics span from system design and databases to AI/ML, security, and DevOps.\n\nThe result is a specialized AI assistant that maintains the base model's general capabilities while excelling in the specific domains covered by the training data. The LoRA adapter is tiny (typically 10-100MB) compared to the full model (tens of GBs), making it easy to store, share, and swap different specializations.",
    "lora, low rank adaptation, parameter efficient fine-tuning, ayarlicazhocam, gorkem ergune, weight matrices, trainable parameters, adapter, llm training")

